#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134703} :activity/id #uuid "6c1ae6c7-6771-5b39-80ac-beb1cde38bb4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050179 :topic/id #uuid "34287e89-f557-56d4-b3da-68793b0edcb7" :topic/math-academy-id 2169} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135062 :tutorial/content "Consider the following data set:\n\n| $x$ | **$100$** | **$110$** | **$120$** | **$130$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $12$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n![](images/8b/8bacbea6990395351aecca8befe779358d57d50f8c14f865d73c2107688fa728.svg)\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "49c9c3ab-40d7-5e87-934c-1c3fcd16b330" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3652 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0ac1d0c2-c862-5d00-b1e2-ce846fd9a9e9" :step/math-academy-id 20307 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134704 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133613 :question/id #uuid "251ec7c6-a050-5deb-8f13-d81f5de4b5c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5610"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048284}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551310}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"} :step/id #uuid "342fc855-0200-53f5-b531-d6f0f6dc12c4" :step/math-academy-id 20308 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134705}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133614 :question/id #uuid "a763a84a-0727-58a3-b524-04afa5735706" :question/math-academy-id "e-5611"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050210}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551337}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"} :step/id #uuid "db5ddd01-2672-598e-9121-f4cf661cc836" :step/math-academy-id 20310 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133905 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133718 :question/id #uuid "29b2a4d7-7e88-5f7a-9a84-ff5315c38f92" :question/math-academy-id "e-9914"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6acfe386-f7e4-54b1-9701-6ff5905b4536" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551350}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Regression Line in Terms of Matrices and Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "af2c310b-8cc3-5365-a974-d47b29df8d67" :step/math-academy-id 37273 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134704}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135142 :tutorial/content "Given a dataset of paired numerical observations $(x,y)$ and the corresponding linear regression model $\\widehat{y} =f(x),$ the **residual** for a particular observation $(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-f(x) \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the *estimated* value predicted by the model from the actual *observed* value. The residuals for the data set we considered earlier are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/515aaaeef2f85bb131564b92bdaef9ef514d05c8028ae85382d231565d490665.png)\n\nIn the past, you may have seen that  calculating a regression line typically means finding a line where the sum of the squared residuals \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2\n$$\n\nis as small as possible. \n\nUsing matrix notation, the sum of the squared residuals can be viewed as \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2 = \\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the condition that the sum of the squared residuals is minimized is equivalent to minimizing $\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.$ This, in turn, is equivalent to minimizing $\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|.$ \n\nTherefore, the problem of minimizing the squared residuals is equivalent to solving the least squares problem for $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eeb5c2f5-02c9-5579-97b5-0393df974a32" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6149 :tutorial/title "Least-Squares vs. Minimizing the Sum of the Squared Residuals"} :step/id #uuid "7c7f5aff-10ef-54b2-b0e0-98663daec8b4" :step/math-academy-id 37328}] :activity/title "Linear Regression With Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134029}]}
